## **7. Database models**

Proposed Prisma models for the module. Check the existing schema before adding these because the college platform may already contain document-related models.

```

enum DocumentStatus {
  DRAFT
  SUBMITTED
  UNDER_REVIEW
  CHANGES_REQUESTED
  APPROVED
  REJECTED
  ISSUED
  ARCHIVED
  REVOKED
}

enum DocumentVisibility {
  PRIVATE
  DEPARTMENT
  CAMPUS
  INSTITUTION
  EXPLICIT_RECIPIENTS
}

enum DocumentAction {
  CREATED
  VIEWED
  UPDATED
  SUBMITTED
  REVIEWED
  APPROVED
  REJECTED
  DOWNLOADED
  PRINTED
  SHARED
  SIGNED
  ISSUED
  ARCHIVED
  RESTORED
  REVOKED
}

model ManagedDocument {
  id              String             @id @default(cuid())
  referenceNumber String?            @unique
  title           String
  description     String?
  category        String
  status          DocumentStatus     @default(DRAFT)
  visibility      DocumentVisibility @default(PRIVATE)

  ownerId         String
  campusId        String?
  departmentId    String?
  templateId      String?

  currentVersion  Int                @default(1)
  issuedAt        DateTime?
  expiresAt       DateTime?
  createdAt       DateTime           @default(now())
  updatedAt       DateTime           @updatedAt

  versions        DocumentVersion[]
  approvals       DocumentApproval[]
  auditLogs       DocumentAuditLog[]
  recipients      DocumentRecipient[]

  @@index([ownerId, status])
  @@index([campusId, departmentId])
  @@index([category, status])
}

model DocumentVersion {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  documentId    String
  version       Int
  storageKey    String
  originalName  String
  mimeType      String
  fileSize      Int
  checksum      String
  uploadedById  String
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())

  document      ManagedDocument @relation(
    fields: [documentId],
    references: [id],
    onDelete: Restrict
  )

  @@unique([documentId, version])
}

model DocumentApproval {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  documentId    String
  reviewerId    String
  stage         Int
  decision      String
  comments      String?
  decidedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())

  document      ManagedDocument @relation(
    fields: [documentId],
    references: [id],
    onDelete: Restrict
  )

  @@index([reviewerId, decision])
}

model DocumentRecipient {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  documentId    String
  recipientId   String
  grantedById   String
  canView       Boolean  @default(true)
  canDownload   Boolean  @default(false)
  expiresAt     DateTime?
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())

  document      ManagedDocument @relation(
    fields: [documentId],
    references: [id],
    onDelete: Restrict
  )

  @@unique([documentId, recipientId])
}

model DocumentAuditLog {
  id            String         @id @default(cuid())
  documentId    String
  actorId       String
  action        DocumentAction
  ipAddress     String?
  userAgent     String?
  metadata      Json?
  createdAt     DateTime       @default(now())

  document      ManagedDocument @relation(
    fields: [documentId],
    references: [id],
    onDelete: Restrict
  )

  @@index([documentId, createdAt])
  @@index([actorId, createdAt])
}

model DocumentTemplate {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  name          String
  category      String
  description   String?
  contentJson   Json
  version       Int      @default(1)
  isPublished   Boolean  @default(false)
  createdById   String
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime @updatedAt

  @@index([category, isPublished])
}

```

Additional production requirements:

- Use private object storage, not publicly accessible upload folders.
- Verify file signatures and MIME types, scan uploads for malware and enforce size limits.
- Store cryptographic checksums and preserve previous versions.
- Use immutable or tamper-evident audit records.
- Use signed, short-lived download URLs after authorization.
- Do not expose private storage keys through API responses.
- Use separate approval records and enforce approval-stage ordering.
- Store actual signature evidence and verification metadata; a typed name or pasted signature image alone is not a legally validated digital signature.

